# React Component Standards

## Component Structure

- Use functional components with hooks
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Keep components small and focused (single responsibility)

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD - Small, focused component
export function TimeSlotButton({ 
  time, 
  isAvailable, 
  onSelect 
}: TimeSlotButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      onClick={() => onSelect(time)}
      disabled={!isAvailable}
      className={cn(
        'px-4 py-2 rounded',
        isAvailable ? 'bg-blue-500' : 'bg-gray-300'
      )}
    >
      {time}
    </button>
  );
}
```

## Props and State

- Use TypeScript interfaces for props
- Prefer composition over prop drilling
- Use React Context for deeply nested state

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
interface BookingFormProps {
  onSubmit: (data: BookingData) => Promise<void>;
  initialDate?: Date;
}

export function BookingForm({ onSubmit, initialDate }: BookingFormProps) {
  // Component logic
}
```

## Performance

- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Extract callbacks with `useCallback` when passing to memoized children
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
const TimeSlotList = React.memo(function TimeSlotList({ 
  slots, 
  onSelect 
}: TimeSlotListProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      {slots.map(slot => (
        <TimeSlotButton key={slot.id} {...slot} onSelect={onSelect} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
});
```
